WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.248
Welcome to your first session with Claude
Code, from K and A Performance.

00:00:05.248 --> 00:00:10.043
By the end, you will know how to open a
project, give Claude a useful brief,

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:13.224
review a change, and check the result
yourself.

00:00:13.224 --> 00:00:16.150
Our practice project is a small reading list.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:21.003
We will ask Claude to make its checkboxes
work and remember their state after

00:00:21.003 --> 00:00:26.413
a refresh. Claude Code can read files, edit
code, and run tools inside a

00:00:26.413 --> 00:00:30.082
project. That makes your instructions
important.

00:00:30.082 --> 00:00:34.749
Start with an outcome, a boundary, and a way
to verify success.

00:00:34.749 --> 00:00:38.882
For example: make the reading checklist
remember my choices.

00:00:38.882 --> 00:00:40.774
Keep the existing design.

00:00:40.774 --> 00:00:43.259
Check that the choices survive a refresh.

00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:46.927
You do not need to memorize every command
before you begin.

00:00:46.927 --> 00:00:50.967
You need a small task and a clear definition
of done.

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:55.240
In the interactive lesson, the practice
terminal is a simulation.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:59.930
It does not run commands on your computer or
send anything to Claude.

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:04.365
Use it to rehearse, then try the same
workflow in your own project.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.629
Let us get the terminal version ready.

00:01:06.629 --> 00:01:10.054
A terminal is simply an app where you type
commands.

00:01:10.054 --> 00:01:12.353
On Windows, open PowerShell.

00:01:12.353 --> 00:01:17.113
On a Mac, open Terminal. Choose your
operating system in the lesson and use

00:01:17.113 --> 00:01:19.818
the linked official installation
instructions.

00:01:19.818 --> 00:01:24.404
The Windows PowerShell installer and the Mac
or Linux shell installer are shown

00:01:24.404 --> 00:01:29.315
on screen. These commands download and run
the official installer, so check

00:01:29.315 --> 00:01:31.683
the address before you use them.

00:01:31.683 --> 00:01:36.896
After installation, open a new terminal and
run claude, followed by two hyphens

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:39.938
and version, to check that it is available.

00:01:39.938 --> 00:01:43.955
You will need access through an eligible
Claude account or another supported

00:01:43.955 --> 00:01:48.495
authentication option. Follow the sign-in
prompts on first launch.

00:01:48.495 --> 00:01:52.964
For a subscription account, choose the Claude
sign-in option.

00:01:52.964 --> 00:01:57.771
API billing is separate. Now extract the
starter ZIP.

00:01:57.771 --> 00:02:03.692
In your terminal, type c d, a space, and the
path to that extracted folder.

00:02:03.692 --> 00:02:06.687
Put the path in quotes if it contains spaces.

00:02:06.687 --> 00:02:09.439
Run claude from inside that folder.

00:02:09.439 --> 00:02:14.210
Read the project trust prompt and continue
only for a folder you recognize.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:19.028
If the command is not found, reopen the
terminal and use the installation
troubleshooting

00:02:19.028 --> 00:02:24.114
link. There is no need to change your entire
computer setup just to complete

00:02:24.114 --> 00:02:25.112
this lesson.

00:02:25.167 --> 00:02:29.416
Before asking Claude to edit, ask it to
explain the project.

00:02:29.416 --> 00:02:34.049
Our starter contains a web page, a
stylesheet, a JavaScript file, and a short

00:02:34.049 --> 00:02:39.238
readme. Ask which file controls the
checkboxes and why selections disappear

00:02:39.238 --> 00:02:44.555
after a refresh. Keep the exploration focused
on this task.

00:02:44.555 --> 00:02:49.513
For an unfamiliar project, slash init can
help draft a CLAUDE dot M D instruction

00:02:49.513 --> 00:02:53.820
file. Review the draft and correct anything
it inferred incorrectly.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.222
The file is guidance, not an enforced
security boundary.

00:02:57.222 --> 00:03:02.156
For this exercise, useful instructions are
simple: preserve the visual design,

00:03:02.156 --> 00:03:06.719
use the existing plain JavaScript, and check
the reading list in a browser.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:11.305
A short, accurate file is more useful than a
long set of generic rules.

00:03:11.305 --> 00:03:14.602
Claude also has automatic memory for learned
patterns.

00:03:14.602 --> 00:03:18.166
That serves a different purpose from the
project rules you maintain.

00:03:18.166 --> 00:03:21.672
You do not need to configure an elaborate
memory system today.

00:03:21.672 --> 00:03:26.397
Start by understanding the files and making
your expectations explicit.

00:03:26.397 --> 00:03:31.018
If the project already has instructions, read
those first and avoid replacing

00:03:31.018 --> 00:03:32.783
them with a generic template.

00:03:32.833 --> 00:03:36.258
Now we turn the request into a small plan.

00:03:36.258 --> 00:03:40.414
In the terminal, Shift plus Tab cycles
through permission modes.

00:03:40.414 --> 00:03:43.247
Stop when the interface shows Plan mode.

00:03:43.247 --> 00:03:47.926
Confirm the displayed mode instead of
assuming one key press always lands in

00:03:47.926 --> 00:03:53.000
the right place. Ask Claude to propose how it
will save checked book identifiers

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.450
and restore them on page load.

00:03:55.450 --> 00:04:00.477
A sensible plan for our project changes the
JavaScript, keeps the layout, and

00:04:00.477 --> 00:04:03.507
checks both selecting and clearing a book.

00:04:03.507 --> 00:04:08.766
Read the plan. If it adds a database, a new
framework, or a sign-in system,

00:04:08.766 --> 00:04:10.798
bring it back to the small task.

00:04:10.798 --> 00:04:14.025
Browser storage is enough for this practice
example.

00:04:14.025 --> 00:04:18.495
Once the plan matches your goal, leave Plan
mode and ask Claude to implement

00:04:18.495 --> 00:04:21.955
it. Read permission requests as they appear.

00:04:21.955 --> 00:04:26.680
A request to edit the expected project file
is different from a request to publish

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.060
a site or delete a folder.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:33.402
If the scope is unclear, stop and ask for an
explanation.

00:04:33.402 --> 00:04:39.857
The useful habit is simple: explore, plan,
implement, verify.

00:04:39.857 --> 00:04:44.281
Planning is especially helpful when you are
new or the change has several moving

00:04:44.281 --> 00:04:45.047
parts.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.457
Claude says the change is complete.

00:04:47.457 --> 00:04:52.020
Now check it. Ask which files changed, what
the checks showed, and what it could

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:57.198
not verify. Review the code changes in your
editor, or use git diff if your

00:04:57.198 --> 00:04:59.938
project is already tracked in Git.

00:04:59.938 --> 00:05:04.837
Our practice project does not require Git or
a build system.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:08.134
Open its index file in a browser.

00:05:08.134 --> 00:05:11.942
Check the first book, refresh, and confirm it
remains checked.

00:05:11.942 --> 00:05:16.888
Uncheck it, refresh again, and confirm it
stays cleared.

00:05:16.888 --> 00:05:20.348
Check a different book to make sure the items
are independent.

00:05:20.348 --> 00:05:22.542
Watch the browser console for errors.

00:05:22.542 --> 00:05:26.803
If your browser restricts storage for local
files, ask Claude to serve this

00:05:26.803 --> 00:05:30.193
folder locally and use that address
consistently.

00:05:30.193 --> 00:05:35.604
On an existing application, use its actual
build and test commands.

00:05:35.604 --> 00:05:40.921
Do not invent an npm test command when the
project has no test script.

00:05:40.921 --> 00:05:46.122
If a check fails, give Claude the exact
observed behavior and any relevant error

00:05:46.122 --> 00:05:50.395
text. Keep secrets out of pasted logs.

00:05:50.395 --> 00:05:54.156
Ask for a focused fix, then repeat the
affected checks.

00:05:54.156 --> 00:05:57.512
Tests, browser checks, and your review work
together.

00:05:57.512 --> 00:06:02.144
They do not guarantee that every possible bug
is gone, but they give you evidence

00:06:02.144 --> 00:06:03.909
about the change you asked for.

00:06:03.967 --> 00:06:09.052
As you work, the conversation fills with
instructions, file contents, and tool

00:06:09.052 --> 00:06:12.164
results. That is context.

00:06:12.164 --> 00:06:17.086
Keep requests focused and avoid dumping
unrelated files into the session.

00:06:17.086 --> 00:06:21.185
Slash context helps you inspect usage.

00:06:21.185 --> 00:06:25.991
Slash compact summarizes a long conversation,
and you can tell it which decisions

00:06:25.991 --> 00:06:31.274
and checks to preserve. When you move to an
unrelated task, slash clear starts

00:06:31.274 --> 00:06:35.325
a fresh conversation. Save any important
decisions first.

00:06:35.325 --> 00:06:39.366
Project instructions are still available when
the new conversation begins.

00:06:39.366 --> 00:06:44.057
There is no universal forty percent target
that guarantees better results.

00:06:44.057 --> 00:06:47.714
The practical question is whether the session
still has the information needed

00:06:47.714 --> 00:06:53.263
for the current task. If Claude starts
repeating an unsuccessful approach, stop,

00:06:53.263 --> 00:06:56.850
explain the mismatch, and narrow the next
step.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:07:01.634
Slash rewind can restore supported
conversation or file checkpoints, but it

00:07:01.634 --> 00:07:04.629
is not a general undo button.

00:07:04.629 --> 00:07:09.621
It cannot undo every shell command or an
external action, such as a published

00:07:09.621 --> 00:07:14.846
deployment. Keep normal backups and use Git
when it fits your project.

00:07:14.846 --> 00:07:20.291
Before ending today, ask Claude for a short
handoff: what changed,

00:07:20.291 --> 00:07:23.809
what passed, and what remains.

00:07:23.809 --> 00:07:27.048
That gives your next session somewhere
sensible to begin.

00:07:27.367 --> 00:07:29.701
You now have the core workflow.

00:07:29.701 --> 00:07:35.471
Define the result, inspect the project,
review a plan, implement a small change,

00:07:35.471 --> 00:07:40.660
and verify it. When you repeat a useful
procedure, a skill can package the
instructions

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:45.583
in a SKILL dot M D file. Full skill
instructions load when used, though the

00:07:45.583 --> 00:07:49.403
skill listing itself can still consume
context.

00:07:49.403 --> 00:07:54.116
Subagents can investigate a focused question
in a separate context and return

00:07:54.116 --> 00:07:58.772
a summary. They are useful for independent
work, but every task does not need

00:07:58.772 --> 00:08:04.183
a team. M C P connects Claude to external
tools and data.

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:08.374
Those connections bring their own permissions
and responsibilities, so add them

00:08:08.374 --> 00:08:13.146
for a specific need. None of these extensions
is required for your first successful

00:08:13.146 --> 00:08:18.626
session. Your next exercise is deliberately
small: ask Claude to show how

00:08:18.626 --> 00:08:23.699
many books remain unread, preserve the
design, and verify that the count changes

00:08:23.699 --> 00:08:26.033
when you select and clear books.

00:08:26.033 --> 00:08:29.260
Use the prompt builder to create a brief for
your own project.

00:08:29.260 --> 00:08:33.544
Then take the same habits with you: a clear
outcome, a sensible boundary, and

00:08:33.544 --> 00:08:35.274
evidence that the result works.

00:08:35.274 --> 00:08:37.445
That is a first session worth repeating.
